```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on leaf_router_0_1, not on spine_router_0_0 as the team suspected.
The bgpd daemon on leaf_router_0_1 crashed at approximately 14:46:45 UTC —
visible in the FRR log as "Client 'bgp' encountered an error and is shutting
down" — which caused the systemd frr.service to enter the "Active: failed" state.
Because bgpd exited, zebra immediately withdrew the one BGP-learned route it had
installed (the 10.0.1.0/24 host prefix), leaving leaf_router_0_1 with only its
three directly-connected entries. With no bgpd process running, leaf_router_0_1
cannot re-establish its eBGP sessions to either spine: both spine_router_0_0
(peer 172.16.0.7, AS 65201) and spine_router_0_1 (peer 172.16.0.11, AS 65201)
show those sessions stuck in the Active state as they keep retrying. Because
neither spine has a live session to leaf_router_0_1, the prefix 10.0.1.0/24 is
never re-advertised up through the fabric, so leaf_router_0_0 has no route to
that subnet and returns "Destination Net Unreachable" for every packet pc_0_0
sends toward pc_0_1. The on-disk frr.conf and daemons file on leaf_router_0_1
are intact and correct (AS 65201, both spine neighbours configured, bgpd=yes),
so the configuration itself is not the issue. The root cause is the bgpd process
crash on leaf_router_0_1. The corrective fix is to restart FRR on leaf_router_0_1
("systemctl restart frr"), which will bring bgpd back up, re-establish the two
eBGP sessions to the spines, and re-advertise 10.0.1.0/24 across the fabric,
restoring end-to-end reachability between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1. spine_router_0_0
does not need to be restarted.
=== END ===
```